                <pb facs="0803" n="713" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg713"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.00/941: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Liberia (Mitchell) to the Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-21T16:00:00-00:44">March 21, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>39. In an endeavor to adjust the Kru Coast situation Barclay is sending a commission composed of Winthrop Travell, one of the American loan
                    officials, with Reverend F. A. K.
                    Russel, a Grebo, and J. F.
                        Coleman, editor of the <hi rendition="#italic">Weekly
                    Mirror</hi>, to investigate and report on the alleged outrages. If conditions do
                    not warrant the presence of soldiers, the Government promises to withdraw them
                    immediately.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mitchell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
